WF-10 — Slack Admin Handler

## Summary

- Inputs: Slack Events API POST at /slack-webhook (url_verification challenge or event_callback with message events from any channel where bot is a member)
- Outputs: HTTP 200 (with challenge echo on verification); dispatches recognized admin commands to WF-11, and Chinmay's free-text replies in active consultation channels to WF-41
- State Transitions: none (delegates; downstream WFs mutate state)
- Calls Sub-Workflows: WF-11 (Command Parser), WF-41 (Admin → User Relay), WF-51 (Send Slack Message — admin feedback on inactive-user relay attempt)
- **Channel-scope design rule (DR-13, see workflow-registry.md):**
  - **User-targeted commands** (carry a `<phone>` argument: APPROVE PAYMENT / APPROVE, REJECT PAYMENT / REJECT, CLOSE CHAT CONSULT / CLOSE CONSULTATION / CLOSE CONSULT, BLOCK, UNBLOCK) are accepted ONLY when typed in the user's `consult-{phone}` Slack channel. WF-10 rejects them (silently dropped or warned in-channel) when typed in `chinmay-admin-commands` or any other channel.
  - **Admin-wide commands** (no phone argument: LIST, STATS, HELP) work in ANY channel.
- **Ambiguities / Notes:**
  - Slack payload field paths use `$json.body.*` (Slack wraps the event under `body`).
  - Bot-loop guard (skip if `body.authorizations[0].user_id == body.event.user`) sits between `Respond 200 OK` and `Extract Required Fields`.
  - WF-10 passes `messageText` (camelCase) downstream to WF-11 and `{phoneNumber, adminMessage}` (camelCase) to WF-41 — input contract uses camelCase consistently across the dispatch boundary.
  - Postgres SQL alias rule: SELECT statements use snake_case column names (no unquoted camelCase aliases — postgres silently lowercases them). camelCase payload shaping happens in a Set node *after* the SELECT, not via projection inside it.
  - WF-41 inactive-user feedback: the FALSE branch of the consultation-active guard routes to a Set → WF-51 admin-feedback chain in the user's `consult-<phone>` channel. Silent drop is not acceptable on the admin-action critical path.

---

## Algorithm

Step 1: Start — receive HTTP POST on /slack-webhook from Slack Events API.
Step 2: Switch on body.type:
  - 'url_verification' → go to Step 3.
  - 'event_callback' → go to Step 4.
  - Anything else (fallback) → go to Step 20 (respond 200 fallback).
Step 3: Respond 200 with {"challenge": body.challenge} (Slack endpoint verification). End.
Step 4: Respond 200 OK immediately (acknowledge to Slack within 3s).
Step 5: Check: body.authorizations[0].user_id != body.event.user (i.e., the message was sent by a human, not the bot itself)?
  - If YES → go to Step 6.
  - If NO (bot's own message) → End (loop prevention).
Step 6: Extract fields: channelId=body.event.channel, adminUserId=body.event.user, messageText=body.event.text, timestamp=body.event.ts.
Step 7: Call Slack API channel.get on channelId to retrieve channel name.
Step 8: Merge the channel info (id, name) with the extracted message fields (combine by id == channelId).
Step 9: Switch on channel name:
  - name == 'chinmay-admin-commands' → go to Step 10 (admin channel branch).
  - name starts with 'consult-' → go to Step 13 (user consultation channel branch).
  - Otherwise → End (channel not relevant).
Step 10: Detect Command (admin channel) — strip mrkdwn formatting; uppercase; trim. Classify:
  - If message starts with one of admin-wide commands [LIST, STATS, HELP] (no phone argument) → set isAdminCommand=true, set commandHint=<keyword>. Allowed here.
  - Else if message starts with one of user-targeted commands [APPROVE, APPROVE PAYMENT, REJECT, REJECT PAYMENT, CLOSE, CLOSE CONSULT, CLOSE CONSULTATION, CLOSE CHAT CONSULT, BLOCK, UNBLOCK] → set wrongChannel=true (user-targeted commands must be typed in the user's consult-{phone} channel, not chinmay-admin-commands).
  - Else → set isAdminCommand=false (chatter; ignore).
Step 11: Check: isAdminCommand == true?
  - If YES → call WF-11 (Command Parser) with payload {messageText, adminUserId, channelId, channelName, commandHint}. End.
  - If wrongChannel == true → post Slack reply to channelId: "⚠️ <commandHint> is a user-targeted command. Please type it inside the user's `consult-{phone}` channel." End.
  - If NO → End (ignore non-command chatter in admin channel).
Step 12: (unused — admin channel has no relay branch.)
Step 13: Detect Command (user consultation channel) — strip mrkdwn formatting; uppercase; trim. Check if message starts with one of [APPROVE, APPROVE PAYMENT, REJECT, REJECT PAYMENT, CLOSE, CLOSE CONSULT, CLOSE CONSULTATION, CLOSE CHAT CONSULT, BLOCK, UNBLOCK, LIST, STATS, HELP]. Set isCommand accordingly.
Step 14: Check: isCommand == true?
  - If YES → call WF-11 (Command Parser) with payload {messageText, adminUserId, channelId, channelName}. End. (User-targeted commands work here per the channel-scope design rule; admin-wide LIST/STATS/HELP also accepted here.)
  - If NO → go to Step 15.
Step 15: SELECT id, status, name, phone_number FROM chinmay_astro.users WHERE slack_channel_id = $channelId LIMIT 1. Postgres node configured with `alwaysOutputData=true` so an empty result emits a row (with all fields null) and routes through the FALSE branch of Step 16 — never a silent halt.
Step 16: Check: user.status == 'consultation_active'?
  - If YES → go to Step 17.
  - If NO  → go to Step 18 (covers both user-not-found, where status is null, and user-in-other-state).
Step 17: Build WF-41 Payload (Set node) — emit {
    phoneNumber: <Load User Status row>.phone_number,
    adminMessage: $('Detect Command - User Channel').item.json.messageText
  }. Then call WF-41 (Admin → User Relay). End. (Chinmay's text reply will be sent to the user via WhatsApp.)
Step 18: Build Admin Feedback (Set node) — emit {
    channelId: $('Detect Command - User Channel').item.json.channelId,
    messageText: "⚠️ User not in active consultation — message not relayed to WhatsApp."
  }. Then call WF-51 (Send Slack Message) — posts the feedback into the same consult-<phone> channel so the admin sees their relay attempt was dropped. End.
Step 20: Respond 200 fallback for unknown body types. End.
